{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "lib": [
      "ES2020",
      "DOM",
      "DOM.Iterable"
    ],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    /*
     * STRICT IS ON BECAUSE ZOD NEEDS strictNullChecks, NOT AS A TIDY-UP.
     *
     * The canvas describes lessons with zod, and zod's inference is built on
     * strictNullChecks. With strict off, `undefined` is assignable to
     * everything, `.optional()` and `.default()` become indistinguishable, and
     * every field of a validated lesson infers as optional. The nine-arm
     * discriminated union of block kinds then has no discriminant it can trust
     * and narrows to `never` — at which point a perfectly valid metric block is
     * reported as not assignable to `never`, and the `ok: false` arm of the
     * validator's result vanishes from the type. Hundreds of errors, not one of
     * them a real defect, all from a single flag.
     *
     * Scoping strict to `src/canvas` in a second config does NOT work, and it
     * is worth writing down why so nobody retries it: `exclude` only filters
     * the ROOT file list. `App.tsx` imports the canvas, so the compiler follows
     * it into the excluded directory anyway and checks it under whichever
     * settings the entry project has. A file graph that spans two projects is
     * checked by both.
     *
     * noImplicitAny stays OFF, and that is the surgical part. Turning full
     * strict on lights up 35 errors, every one of them noImplicitAny and every
     * one of them in `src/data/curriculum.ts` — the original dashboard, which
     * is out of scope here. NONE of them are null-safety. So the canvas gets
     * the flag it actually needs and the dashboard is not touched to pay for
     * it. Turning this on later is a real improvement, and a separate job.
     */
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "noUnusedLocals": false,
    "noUnusedParameters": false,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true
  },
  "include": [
    "src",
    /* `cto` — the engineering environment this repo is built IN. It ships to
       nobody, but it decides what may be called known, so it is typechecked
       with everything else rather than trusted. */
    "cto",
    /* A test under `src` imports `server/handler.ts`, so TypeScript follows the
       chain into `server/` and typechecks files this project does not list.
       Those files depend on the ambient Node declarations that live beside
       them, and without this line the src project reports "Cannot find module
       'node:crypto'" for code it was never asked to own. Declaring the types
       file is smaller and truer than widening the project to all of `server`. */
    "server/node.d.ts"
  ]
}